  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAs was a possibility advertised by some ensemble and\ndeterministic guidance suite members, it appears that the\neastern solutions will win out with tonight's rain event. The\nconsensus shows that the upstream short wave trough or \"kicker\"\nwill be just speedy enough to push the best forcing for ascent\nand IVT plume too far east to give anyone a good soaking\nrainfall. The only location where it still remains in the realm\nof possibility is the immediate coast.\n\nMaximum rainfall amounts should top out around a half inch on \nthe immediate coast with some locally higher amounts possible. \nFarther inland, less than a quarter inch is expected, with some \nlocations in western and northern NH remaining dry or mainly \ndry. This goes for the western ME mountains as well.\n\nDrought begets drought as some may say.\n\nRegarding temperatures, expect highs ranging from the low to \nmid 50s north, to the low to mid 60s south and along the coast. \n\nWesterly wind gusts of 20 to 30 MPH possible Monday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAfter a brief stretch of dry and cooler weather Tuesday-Wednesday, \neyes turn to the next system expected to impact the Northeast around \nThanksgiving day, although exact timing and duration remains \nuncertain. Guidance currently indicates rather favorable dynamics \nfor a strengthening coastal low, with a dual jet streak structure off \nthe east coast. The odds are increasing for a strong storm from \nThanksgiving into the holiday weekend, which will impact the travel \nrush.\n\nA pretty substantial shift across all guidance/ensemble members \nsince yesterday towards a further north surface low track, with all \nGEFS/EPS members now near/inside of the benchmark. The cause? The \nupstream ridge has trended stronger/further east, which is leading \nto a further northwest track along with stronger dynamics/forcing in \nplay. Some of the ensemble members are also quite strong; about a \nthird are below 990 mb.\n\nGiven the storm track shifts, as well as a further east ridge, \nexpecting much less of a cold dome to start the event. As such, a \nsignificant trend overnight to a warmer system. In addition, the \norientation of the pressure gradient suggests much more \neasterly/onshore flow off the relatively milder ocean (as opposed to \na colder north wind). As such, chances for snow are quickly fading \noutside of the mountains and interior, but even there, \nsleet/freezing rain seems more likely as models are insistent on a \nwarm nose arriving with the precipitation. Additional hazards will \nbe strong easterly winds and the threat for coastal flooding \n(partially due to the strong onshore flow, but also tides will be \nastronomically high as a baseline). A soaking cold rain is looking \nincreasingly likely, but with the antecedent dry conditions, not \nconcerned about excessive rainfall/on land flooding at this\ntime.",
